                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "While the first game runs with no issues on my PC, the second has, for some strange reason, the text playing at lightspeed, making it unplayable. Had to resort to a video walkthrough to refresh my memories. Honestly, they are pretty much as I remembered them from their original releases.\n\nThe visual style is simple but fairly consistent; what could have been done even a little better are the animations, at least the walking cycles, which are too limited and ridiculous.\n\nMany puzzles are \"crazy\", as expected from surreal and absurdist games like these, yet manage to not feel like the only way to find a solution is to try every object in the inventory with other objects or with the scenery; if you do that, however, there are many unique responses to be read.\n\n[b]Time Gentlemen, Please![/b] has a puzzle related to clocks that is very clever, possibly my favourite in both games, but a part related to a game within the game (not spoiling anything further) deserves a mention too. The second game is centered on time travel and manipulation shenanigans, between rifts in space-time and a device that can alter an object to make it newer or older; such puzzles show a strong inspiration from [b]Day of the Tentacle[/b], even the same font is used for text here. The presence of a map that allows you to quickly fast-travel to any discovered location is quite welcome.\n\nThe games poke a lot of fun at adventure game tropes, logic and conventions, but the constant leaning on the fourth wall may wear thin, especially in the second game, which is twice as long as the first. Combine that with a strong dose of crass humour; at least to my taste, the jokes don't always land and the characters can easily go from funny to insufferably stupid to just insufferable, depending on the scenes.\n\nIn short, [b]Ben There, Dan That![/b] (how great is such a title, by the way?) is funnier because it's shorter and doesn't overstay its welcome, but as an adventure game, the sequel fares better. But for such a low price, even considering that the first game was originally freeware, it's really hard to go wrong with them.",
